        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: To find the integer roots of the polynomial \\(x^4 + 5x^3 + 9x^2 - x - 14 = 0\\), we can use the Rational Root Theorem, which states that any rational root, expressed as a fraction \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\), must have \\(p\\) as a factor of the constant term (here, -14) and \\(q\\) as a factor of the leading coefficient (here, 1)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 498,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 500,
        "char_start": 326,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 165,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 166,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Since the leading coefficient is 1, \\(q\\) must be \\(\\pm 1\\). Therefore, the possible rational roots are the factors of -14, which are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 7, \\pm 14\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 610,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 611,
        "char_start": 502,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 189,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 190,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: We will test these possible roots by substituting them into the polynomial to see if they yield zero."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 724,
